text: >-
  Forensic Review of Video Metadata and File Hash

  This document summarizes the metadata and file characteristics of the video
  file reviewed from a Canon XA55

  camera. The purpose is to explain, in plain language, whether the video
  appears to be an original recording and

  to explain the significance of the file hash and metadata values.

  Overall Assessment

  Based on the metadata reviewed, the file appears highly consistent with an
  original, camera-generated

  recording from a Canon XA55 professional camcorder. The file contains native
  Canon camera metadata,

  camera-specific compression information, a camera serial number, and an
  original timecode track. There are

  no indications that the file was exported or re-encoded by common editing
  software such as Adobe Premiere,

  Final Cut, CapCut, FFmpeg, or similar programs.

  In plain language: there is no obvious sign that this file was created later
  from another video or altered through

  editing software. The metadata instead looks like the type of information that
  is normally written directly by the

  camera when the video is first recorded.

  What a File Hash Is

  A file hash is a unique digital fingerprint for a file. A SHA-256 hash takes
  every single byte in the file and turns it

  into a long string of letters and numbers.

  If even one pixel, one frame, one piece of metadata, or one character changes
  in the file, the hash will change

  completely. Two files with the exact same SHA-256 hash are considered to be
  exactly identical.

  In plain language: a hash is like comparing fingerprints. If the fingerprints
  match, it is the same file. If they do not

  match, something changed.

  Explanation of the "Minor Version 0.0.1" Field

  The metadata includes the line "Minor Version: 0.0.1." This does not indicate
  that the file is fake, altered, or not

  original.

  "Minor Version" is simply a technical version number used inside the MP4 file
  format. Different cameras and

  software write different values there. Canon cameras commonly use "0.0.1" as
  part of their MP4 structure.

  In plain language: this number is similar to a version number printed on the
  inside of a product. It describes the

  type of MP4 container the camera created. It does not mean "copy number one"
  or "not the original."

  Items That Might Look "Off" But Are Not

  The metadata includes a warning that says "Suspicious MakerNotes offset." This
  warning is generally minor

  and is common when reading private Canon metadata. It usually occurs because
  of the way Canon stores

  hidden camera information inside the file. The remainder of the Canon metadata
  is intact and internally

  consistent.


  The file also shows "File Creation Date/Time" in November 2025 even though the
  video itself was recorded in

  September 2025. This is not the recording date. It is simply the date the file
  was copied or saved onto that

  particular computer.

  Why the Metadata Shows September 9 Instead of

  September 10

  The camera recorded the video on September 9, 2025 at 10:35:04 PM local time.
  The MP4 container then

  stores another timestamp in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC).

  The metadata shows: Date/Time Original: 2025:09:09 22:35:04 Create Date:
  2025:09:10 03:35:05 Time Zone:

  -05:00

  Because the local time zone was UTC-5, adding five hours moves the time from
  late on September 9 to early

  on September 10.

  In plain language: the video was recorded at approximately 10:35 PM on
  September 9 in the local time zone,

  which equals approximately 3:35 AM on September 10 in UTC. The metadata is
  therefore internally consistent

  and does not indicate tampering.

  Key Findings Supporting Originality

  Camera identified as Canon XA55 professional camcorder Native Canon compressor
  string present Original

  camera serial number present Original timecode track present No evidence of
  Adobe Premiere, Final Cut,

  FFmpeg, or other editing software Timestamps and time zone information are
  internally consistent Video

  settings are consistent with direct camera recording: 1920×1080, 23.976 fps,
  38 Mbps

  Conclusion

  Based on the metadata and structure of the file, the video appears highly
  likely to be an original recording

  generated directly by a Canon XA55 camera. There are no obvious signs of
  editing, re-exporting, or

  recompression. The unusual-looking metadata values, including "Minor Version:
  0.0.1" and the September 9

  versus September 10 dates, have ordinary technical explanations and do not
  suggest the file is not original.
